    "content": "[PDF PAGE 1]\nWhat is Candle Range Theory – CRT\n\n\nHey folks! this blog post is dedicated to the viral trading concept named the Candle Range Theory – CRT.\n\nBeing an ICT trader we all know that why price moves, its because of liquidity and inefficiency. And the\nCRT is also based on the liquidity.\n\nBut before going forward I want to give a short disclaimer that the candle range theory is not an ICT\nconcept but it is derived from the ICT trading concepts like the ICT Liquidity Sweep, ICT Power of\n3 and ICT Session High/Low Liquidity.\n\nWhat is Candle Range?\n\nBefore understanding the Candle Range you should have a basic knowledge about the Candlestick.\n\nEvery candlestick on a higher time frame represents a range on the lower time frame.\n\nSo the high of a candlestick represents the high of a range on lower time-frame which is called CRT-High\nwhile the low of candlestick represents the low of range on lower time-frame which is called CRT-Low\nand ICT has explained this too earlier.\n\n[PDF PAGE 2]\nWhat is Candle Range Theory – CRT?\n\nCandle range theory is based on the liquidity raid of the previous candle range and then run to the next\nliquidity level.\n\nLike if price grabs the liquidity of previous candle’s low then we can expect the price to run towards the\nhigh of candle range and vice versa.\n\nBullish – Candle Range Theory Model\n\nIf price is at a key support level of higher time-frame then you can look for a bullish CRT model.\n\nFor a bullish CRT model you have to mark the high and low of candlestick closed at support, now wait for\nthe next candlestick to grab the low of previous candle and close above the low.\n\nAfter the above phenomenon you can wait for another candlestick to close above the high of candlestick\nwhich raided the low or simply you can look for an ICT Market Structure Shift on the lower time-frame\nand then execute a buy trade on retest.\n\nThis all phenomenon can happen with only 3 candlesticks but sometime price may accumulate after\nraiding the low so there can be more then 3 candlestick too.\n\n[PDF PAGE 3]\nIn this case your stop-loss will be below the low of candlestick which raided the liquidity or below the\nMSS-low while for for take profit you can target the CRT-High or next liquidity levels.\n\nBearish – Candle Range Theory Model\n\nWhen the price is at a key resistance level on a higher time frame, you can look for a bearish CRT model.\n\nFor a bearish CRT model, begin by marking the high and low of the candlestick that closes at the\nresistance level. Then, wait for the next candlestick to grab the high of the previous candle and close\nbelow that high.\n\nAfter this setup, you can wait for the following candlestick to close below the low of the candlestick that\ntook out the high. Alternatively, you can look for an ICT Market Structure Shift on a lower time frame,\nthen enter a sell trade on the retest.\n\nThis entire scenario can unfold within just three candlesticks, but sometimes the price may consolidate\nafter grabbing the high, so it could involve more than three candlesticks.\n\n\n\n\nFor risk management, your stop-loss should be placed above the high of the candlestick that grabbed\nliquidity or above the MSS-high. For your take profit, target the CRT-Low or the next liquidity levels\nbelow.\n\n[PDF PAGE 4]\nHigh Probability CRT Setups\n\nHigh probability CRT setups can be found during the ICT Kill Zones or ICT Sessions Raid.\n\nICT PO3 vs CRT\n\nCandle range theory is basically the application of ICT Power of 3.\n\nICT PO3 states that price will first accumulate then manipulate by moving in a direction and taking short-\nterm liquidity then it distributes by moving to the opposite direction.\n\nAnd according to the CRT price accumulates and takes the liquidity of CRT-High/Low the moves toward\nthe opposite side.\n\nThe CRT-High/Low can also be the ICT previous day high/low or previous session high/low.\n\nConclusion\n\nCandle range theory is nothing new, this is what ICT taught us years ago and people just renamed it and\npacked it into their label claiming a new trading strategy."
